#
# Test vector function HELP entries
#
# HELP category should list all vector functions
HELP 'Vector Functions';
source_category_name	name	is_it_category
Vector Functions	VECTOR_DIM	N
Vector Functions	VEC_DISTANCE	N
Vector Functions	VEC_FROMTEXT	N
Vector Functions	VEC_TOTEXT	N
# HELP category should list vector data type, index, variables, and status topics
HELP 'Vector';
source_category_name	name	is_it_category
Vector	VECTOR COLUMNS	N
Vector	VECTOR INDEXES	N
Vector	VECTOR STATUS	N
Vector	VECTOR VARIABLES	N
# Individual function topics
HELP 'VEC_DISTANCE';
name	description	example
VEC_DISTANCE	Syntax:
VEC_DISTANCE(v1, v2 [, metric])

Computes the distance between two vectors v1 and v2. Both arguments
must be VECTOR values with the same number of dimensions.

The optional metric argument specifies the distance metric. Supported
values are 'EUCLIDEAN' (default), 'COSINE' and 'INNER_PRODUCT'.

Returns a DOUBLE value representing the distance. Smaller values
indicate more similar vectors. For 'INNER_PRODUCT' the returned value
is the negative inner product (multiply by -1 to obtain the true dot
product), so that ORDER BY ... ASC still yields the most similar
vectors.

Shorthand forms VEC_DISTANCE_EUCLIDEAN(v1, v2),
VEC_DISTANCE_COSINE(v1, v2) and VEC_DISTANCE_INNER_PRODUCT(v1, v2) are
also available.

When used with ORDER BY ... LIMIT, the optimizer can accelerate the
query using a vector index (HNSW) if one exists on the column.

	mysql> SELECT VEC_DISTANCE(VEC_FROMTEXT('[1,2,3]'), VEC_FROMTEXT('[4,5,6]'));
        -> 5.196152422706632
mysql> SELECT VEC_DISTANCE(VEC_FROMTEXT('[1,0]'), VEC_FROMTEXT('[0,1]'), 'COSINE');
        -> 1

HELP 'VEC_FROMTEXT';
name	description	example
VEC_FROMTEXT	Syntax:
VEC_FROMTEXT(text_string)

Converts a text representation of a vector to the internal binary
VECTOR format. The input should be a comma-separated list of numbers
enclosed in square brackets, e.g. '[1.0, 2.0, 3.0]'.

Aliases: TO_VECTOR(), STRING_TO_VECTOR().

	mysql> SELECT VEC_TOTEXT(VEC_FROMTEXT('[1, 2, 3]'));
        -> [1.000000,2.000000,3.000000]

HELP 'VEC_TOTEXT';
name	description	example
VEC_TOTEXT	Syntax:
VEC_TOTEXT(vector_value)

Converts a binary VECTOR value to its text representation. The output
is a comma-separated list of floating-point numbers enclosed in square
brackets.

Aliases: FROM_VECTOR(), VECTOR_TO_STRING().

	mysql> SELECT VEC_TOTEXT(col) FROM t WHERE id = 1;
        -> [1.000000,2.000000,3.000000]

HELP 'VECTOR_DIM';
name	description	example
VECTOR_DIM	Syntax:
VECTOR_DIM(vector_value)

Returns the number of dimensions of a VECTOR value as an integer.

	mysql> SELECT VECTOR_DIM(VEC_FROMTEXT('[1, 2, 3]'));
        -> 3

# Vector column and index topics
HELP 'VECTOR COLUMNS';
name	description	example
VECTOR COLUMNS	The VECTOR data type stores fixed-length arrays of single-precision
floating-point numbers. Use it to store embedding vectors generated by
machine learning models.

Syntax:
  column_name VECTOR(dimension)

where dimension is the number of elements (1 to 16383).

VECTOR columns store data in a compact binary format. Use VEC_FROMTEXT()
to convert a text representation (e.g. '[1.0, 2.0, 3.0]') into the
binary format for INSERT, and VEC_TOTEXT() to convert back for display.

VECTOR columns can be indexed with a VECTOR INDEX to enable approximate
nearest neighbor (ANN) search. See [HELP VECTOR INDEXES].

	CREATE TABLE items (
  id INT PRIMARY KEY,
  embedding VECTOR(128)
);

INSERT INTO items VALUES (1, VEC_FROMTEXT('[0.1, 0.2, ...]'));

SELECT id, VEC_TOTEXT(embedding) FROM items WHERE id = 1;

HELP 'VECTOR INDEXES';
name	description	example
VECTOR INDEXES	A VECTOR INDEX creates an HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World)
graph index on a VECTOR column to enable fast approximate nearest
neighbor (ANN) search.

Syntax:
  VECTOR INDEX [index_name] (column_name) [DISTANCE=metric] [M=value]
    [EF_CONSTRUCTION=value]

where metric is EUCLIDEAN (default), COSINE or INNER_PRODUCT, M controls the number
of neighbor connections per node (default 6, range 3-200), and
EF_CONSTRUCTION controls the search width during index construction
(default 10, range 5-1000). Larger EF_CONSTRUCTION values produce
higher quality graphs but slower index creation.

Creating a vector index:

o With CREATE TABLE:

  CREATE TABLE items (
    id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    embedding VECTOR(128),
    VECTOR INDEX idx_emb (embedding) DISTANCE=COSINE EF_CONSTRUCTION=100
  );

o With CREATE INDEX:

  CREATE VECTOR INDEX idx_emb ON items (embedding) DISTANCE=COSINE;

o With ALTER TABLE:

  ALTER TABLE items ADD VECTOR INDEX idx_emb (embedding);

The optimizer uses the vector index when a query contains
VEC_DISTANCE() in ORDER BY with a LIMIT clause:

  SELECT id FROM items
  ORDER BY VEC_DISTANCE(embedding, VEC_FROMTEXT('[...]'), 'COSINE')
  LIMIT 10;

The session variable vidx_hnsw_ef_search controls the search accuracy
vs speed tradeoff at query time (default 20, higher = more accurate).
See [HELP VECTOR VARIABLES].

To drop a vector index, use ALTER TABLE or DROP INDEX:

  ALTER TABLE items DROP INDEX idx_emb;
  DROP INDEX idx_emb ON items;

	CREATE TABLE items (
  id INT PRIMARY KEY,
  embedding VECTOR(3),
  VECTOR INDEX idx_emb (embedding) DISTANCE=COSINE M=6 EF_CONSTRUCTION=50
);

INSERT INTO items VALUES
  (1, VEC_FROMTEXT('[1,0,0]')),
  (2, VEC_FROMTEXT('[0,1,0]')),
  (3, VEC_FROMTEXT('[0,0,1]'));

SELECT id FROM items
ORDER BY VEC_DISTANCE(embedding, VEC_FROMTEXT('[1,0,0]'), 'COSINE')
LIMIT 2;

# Vector variables and status topics
HELP 'VECTOR VARIABLES';
name	description	example
VECTOR VARIABLES	The vector index plugin (vidx) exposes several system variables for
tuning behavior. Plugin variables use the vidx_ prefix.

Session Variables (SET [SESSION]):

o vidx_hnsw_ef_search (default 20, range 1-10000)
  Controls ANN search accuracy vs speed. Defines the minimal number of
  result candidates to examine for ORDER BY ... LIMIT N queries. Larger
  values give more accurate results but slower queries. The search will
  never examine fewer candidates than this value, even if LIMIT is
  smaller.

o vidx_hnsw_default_m (default 6, range 3-200)
  Default M (neighbor connections per HNSW node) for newly created
  vector indexes. Larger values increase index size and memory usage
  but improve search accuracy. Can be overridden per-index with the
  M=value option in CREATE VECTOR INDEX.

o vidx_hnsw_default_ef_construction (default 10, range 5-1000)
  Default EF_CONSTRUCTION for newly created vector indexes. Controls
  the search width during HNSW graph construction. Larger values
  produce higher quality graphs but slower index creation. Can be
  overridden per-index with the EF_CONSTRUCTION=value option in
  CREATE VECTOR INDEX.

o vidx_ddl_threads (default 4, range 1-64)
  Controls worker concurrency for every in-memory window of a synchronous
  vector-index COPY DDL build. Set to 1 for serial build. This setting is
  independent of innodb_ddl_threads.

o vidx_default_distance (default EUCLIDEAN)
  Default distance metric for newly created vector indexes. Supported
  values: EUCLIDEAN, COSINE, INNER_PRODUCT. Can be overridden per-index with the
  DISTANCE=metric option in CREATE VECTOR INDEX.

Global Variables (SET GLOBAL):

o vidx_disabled (default ON)
  Master switch to enable/disable the vector index feature. Set to OFF
  to enable vector indexes.

o vidx_hnsw_cache_size (default 16MB)
  Upper limit in bytes for one HNSW vector index runtime cache. During
  synchronous COPY DDL it is reused only as a soft graph-window hint,
  evaluated between complete micro-batches. An in-flight batch may exceed
  it and a small value never prevents index creation.

o vidx_hnsw_lock_timeout_ms (default 1000, range 1-50000)
  Lock wait timeout in milliseconds for HNSW graph table operations on
  replica apply threads. Prevents a single hot graph row from stalling
  replication apply progress.

	-- Tune ef_search for higher accuracy before running ANN queries:
SET SESSION vidx_hnsw_ef_search = 100;

SELECT id FROM items
ORDER BY VEC_DISTANCE(embedding, VEC_FROMTEXT('[1,0,0]'), 'COSINE')
LIMIT 10;

-- Check current settings:
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'vidx%';

HELP 'VECTOR STATUS';
name	description	example
VECTOR STATUS	The vector index plugin exposes status variables for monitoring.
All are global counters viewable via SHOW GLOBAL STATUS LIKE 'Vidx_%'.

Cache Performance:
  Vidx_load_node_hits      HNSW graph node cache hits
  Vidx_load_node_misses    HNSW graph node cache misses
  Vidx_cache_usage         Current HNSW cache memory usage in bytes
  Vidx_load_vec_misses     Vector data cache misses

DML Counters:
  Vidx_query_count         Total ANN queries served
  Vidx_insert_count        Total vector index insert operations
  Vidx_update_count        Total vector index update operations
  Vidx_invalidate_count    Total vector index delete/invalidate ops

Replication Health (non-zero on replica may indicate graph divergence):
  Vidx_save_retry_dup_key                   Retried inserts due to dup key
  Vidx_invalidate_skip_not_found            Skipped deletes (row not found)
  Vidx_invalidate_skip_read_timeout         Skipped due to read lock timeout
  Vidx_invalidate_skip_update_timeout       Skipped due to update lock timeout
  Vidx_save_target_skip_lock_timeout        Target save skipped (lock timeout)
  Vidx_save_neighbor_skip_lock_timeout      Neighbor save skipped (lock timeout)

Independent-Transaction DML Health:
  Vidx_indep_dml_commit         Successful independent-trx commits
  Vidx_indep_dml_commit_error   Failed independent-trx commits
  Vidx_indep_dml_deadlock       Deadlocks in independent-trx DML

Synchronous COPY DDL:
  Vidx_ddl_direct_builds_active       Current direct builds
  Vidx_ddl_build_windows              Persisted non-empty windows
  Vidx_ddl_parallel_batches           Completed W>1 micro-batches
  Vidx_ddl_parallel_load_rounds       Leader load barriers with I/O
  Vidx_ddl_parallel_loaded_nodes      Historical nodes loaded by leader
  Vidx_ddl_build_error_count          Internally failed direct builds

	SHOW GLOBAL STATUS LIKE 'Vidx_%';

-- Check cache hit rate:
SELECT
  (SELECT VARIABLE_VALUE FROM performance_schema.global_status
   WHERE VARIABLE_NAME = 'Vidx_load_node_hits') AS hits,
  (SELECT VARIABLE_VALUE FROM performance_schema.global_status
   WHERE VARIABLE_NAME = 'Vidx_load_node_misses') AS misses;

# Alias keywords should resolve to the correct topic
HELP 'VEC_DISTANCE_EUCLIDEAN';
name	description	example
VEC_DISTANCE	Syntax:
VEC_DISTANCE(v1, v2 [, metric])

Computes the distance between two vectors v1 and v2. Both arguments
must be VECTOR values with the same number of dimensions.

The optional metric argument specifies the distance metric. Supported
values are 'EUCLIDEAN' (default), 'COSINE' and 'INNER_PRODUCT'.

Returns a DOUBLE value representing the distance. Smaller values
indicate more similar vectors. For 'INNER_PRODUCT' the returned value
is the negative inner product (multiply by -1 to obtain the true dot
product), so that ORDER BY ... ASC still yields the most similar
vectors.

Shorthand forms VEC_DISTANCE_EUCLIDEAN(v1, v2),
VEC_DISTANCE_COSINE(v1, v2) and VEC_DISTANCE_INNER_PRODUCT(v1, v2) are
also available.

When used with ORDER BY ... LIMIT, the optimizer can accelerate the
query using a vector index (HNSW) if one exists on the column.

	mysql> SELECT VEC_DISTANCE(VEC_FROMTEXT('[1,2,3]'), VEC_FROMTEXT('[4,5,6]'));
        -> 5.196152422706632
mysql> SELECT VEC_DISTANCE(VEC_FROMTEXT('[1,0]'), VEC_FROMTEXT('[0,1]'), 'COSINE');
        -> 1

